<p><a name=3D"documents-revisions-renditions"></a></p>
<h2 id=3D"documents,-revisions,-renditions">Documents, Revisions, Rendition=
s</h2>
<p>A single document is broken down into three separate layers. These layer=
s are formally designated Document, Revision, and Rendition. The tree below=
 shows the relationship between the layers.</p>
<p><img src=3D"https://sdk.onbase.com/rest/OnBase/Foundation26.1/document/p=
rogrammers-guide/images/UnityRevRend.gif" alt=3D"Document-Revision-Renditio=
n-Structure">
<a name=3D"document"></a></p>
<h3 id=3D"document">Document</h3>
<p>A <code>document</code> resource provides metadata about the document. T=
o request a document, you must specify the <code>{documentId}</code> for th=
at document.</p>
<p>The endpoint for retrieving metadata for a document is <code>/documents/=
{documentId}</code>.</p>
<p><a name=3D"revision"></a></p>
<h3 id=3D"revision">Revision</h3>
<p>A revision is a container for the contents of a document. A new revision=
 of the document is stored each time a document is changed. A document cons=
ists of one or more revisions. To request a specific revision of a document=
, you must specify the <code>{revisionId}</code> for that revision. If an O=
nBase system does not have EDM Services or revisions is not configured for =
a given document type, the concept of revisions still applies. Every docume=
nt still has at least one revision.</p>
<p>The endpoint for retrieivng the list of revisions of a document is <code=
>/documents/{documentId}/revisions</code>. The endpoint for retrieving a sp=
ecific revision is <code>/documents/{documentId}/revisions/{revisionId}</co=
de>.
<a name=3D"latest-revision"></a></p>
<h4 id=3D"latest-revision">Latest Revision</h4>
<p>To get the latest revision of a document, you can request the <code>late=
st</code> resource in place of <code>{revisionId}</code>.</p>
<p>Example: <code>/documents/{documentId}/revisions/latest</code>
<a name=3D"rendition"></a></p>
<h3 id=3D"rendition">Rendition</h3>
<p>A rendition is a version of an original document that is saved in a diff=
erent File Format. A document revision consists of one or more renditions. =
The <code>rendition</code> resource provides metadata about the rendition. =
To request a specific rendition of a document revision, you must specify th=
e <code>{fileTypeId}</code> for that rendition.</p>
<p>The endpoint for retrieving the list of renditions of a revision is <cod=
e>/documents/{documentId}/revisions/{revisionId}/renditions</code>. The end=
point for retrieving a specific rendition is <code>/documents/{documentId}/=
revisions/{revisionId}/renditions/{fileTypeId}</code>.
<a name=3D"default-rendition"></a></p>
<h4 id=3D"default-rendition">Default Rendition</h4>
<p>To get the default rendition of a document revision, you can request the=
 <code>default</code> resource in place of <code>{fileTypeId}</code>.</p>
<p>Example: <code>/documents/{documentId}/revisions/{revisionId}/renditions=
/default</code></p>
<p>The default rendition can be used in conjunction with the latest revisio=
n. This is the best way to get the document, unless more specific revisions=
 and renditions are needed.</p>
<p>Example: <code>/documents/{documentId}/revisions/latest/renditions/defau=
lt</code></p>
<p><a name=3D"document-content"></a></p>
<h2 id=3D"content">Content</h2>
<p>The <code>content</code> resource provides document bytes for the specif=
ied rendition.</p>
<p>The endpoint is <code>/documents/{documentId}/revisions/{revisionId}/ren=
ditions/{fileTypeId}/content</code>.</p>
<p><a name=3D"accept-header"></a></p>
<h3 id=3D"accept-header">Accept Header</h3>
<p>The <code>Accept</code> header is used to request a particular file form=
at. The <code>content</code> resource supports <a href=3D"https://tools.iet=
f.org/html/rfc7231#section-3.4.1">proactive negotiation</a>.
Content negotiation allows the client to specify the list of formats they w=
ould prefer to receive or specify which to ignore if unable to process part=
icular formats. If the document cannot be retrieved in any of the given for=
mats, <code>406 Not Acceptable</code> will be returned.
<a name=3D"media-types"></a></p>
<h4 id=3D"media-types">Media Types</h4>
<p><a href=3D"https://tools.ietf.org/html/rfc6838">Media types</a> are used=
 to specify the file format of the document.</p>
<p>To get the default media type of the document, either omit the <code>Acc=
ept</code> header or use the full wild card media type: <code>*/*</code>.</=
p>
<p>Subtype wildcards can be used to retrieve the best media type of a parti=
cular type. The following subtype wildcards are supported by the content en=
d point: <code>image/*</code>, <code>application/*</code>, and <code>text/*=
</code>.</p>
<p>You can try to retrieve a document in a specific media type. The followi=
ng media types can be requested: <code>image/tiff</code>, <code>image/png</=
code>, <code>image/jpeg</code>, <code>application/pdf</code>, and <code>tex=
t/plain</code>.</p>
<p>If the requested media type is not supported or a document cannot be ret=
rieved as the specified media type, a <code>406 Not Acceptable</code> will =
be returned.</p>
<p>Supported media types are dependent on the document's original file type=
 as well as other request parameters. Review the <a href=3D"https://sdk.onb=
ase.com/rest/OnBase/Foundation26.1/document/programmers-guide/document-retr=
ieval.html#supported-media-types">Supported Media Types</a> section for mor=
e detail.</p>
<p><a name=3D"large-file-downloads"></a></p>
<h3 id=3D"large-file-downloads">Large File Downloads</h3>
<p>For very large files, the document can be retrieved in chunks using spec=
ial headers. The=20
specific bytes retrieved with each request can be specified by range header=
s and ETag headers=20
can be used to ensure that the bytes that are being retrieved are from the =
exact same rendering=20
of the document.</p>
<p><a name=3D"range-headers"></a></p>
<h4 id=3D"range-headers">Range Headers</h4>
<p>To specify a specific set of bytes to be retrieved, the request header <=
code>Range</code> and the response=20
headers <code>Content-Range</code> and <code>Accept-Ranges</code> are used.=
 If a range is provided in the <code>Range</code>=20
header that cannot be returned, a <code>416 Range Not Satisfiable</code> er=
ror will be returned.</p>
<p><a name=3D"etag-headers"></a></p>
<h4 id=3D"etag-headers">ETag Headers</h4>
<p>To ensure that the bytes are coming from the same rendering of the docum=
ent, the response header=20
<code>ETag</code> and the request header <code>If-Match</code> are used. Th=
ese contain an ETag or unique identifier=20
for the file. If the file matching the ETag provided is not found, the serv=
er will return <code>412=20
Precondition Failed</code>.</p>
<p><a name=3D"content-resizing-parameters"></a></p>
<h3 id=3D"content-resizing-parameters">Content Resizing Parameters</h3>
<p>A document can be resized by including the query parameter <code>fit</co=
de> along with the parameters=20
<code>height</code> and/or <code>width</code>. There are four options for t=
he <code>fit</code> parameter: <code>HEIGHT</code>, <code>WIDTH</code>,=20
<code>STRETCH</code>, or <code>BOTH</code>. The next two tables describe th=
e behavior for each of these four options.</p>
<p><strong> The content resizing parameters can only be used when retrievin=
g the document as an image </strong></p>
<table>
<thead>
<tr>
<th><code>fit</code></th>
<th>Description</th>
</tr>
</thead>
<tbody>
<tr>
<td><code>HEIGHT</code></td>
<td>You must include the <code>height</code> query parameter for this optio=
n. This will adjust the height of the document to what you requested and ad=
just the width to match the original aspect ratio.</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td><code>WIDTH</code></td>
<td>You must include the <code>width</code> query parameter for this option=
. This will adjust the width of the document to what you requested and adju=
st the height to match the original aspect ratio.</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td><code>STRETCH</code></td>
<td>You must include both the <code>height</code> and <code>width</code> qu=
ery parameter for this option. This sets the height and the width to exactl=
y what you requested, the original aspect ratio will not be respected.</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td><code>BOTH</code></td>
<td>You must include both the <code>height</code> and <code>width</code> qu=
ery parameter for this option. This will look at the newly requested height=
 and width and determine which of the two uses a smaller scale. It will the=
n resize the document based on that scale. The original aspect ratio will b=
e respected. In the example below the requested width results in a smaller =
scale so it is used to resize the document and the requested height is igno=
red.</td>
</tr>
</tbody>
</table>
<table>
<thead>
<tr>
<th><code>fit</code></th>
<th>Initial Size</th>
<th>Query String</th>
<th>Adjusted Size</th>
</tr>
</thead>
<tbody>
<tr>
<td><code>HEIGHT</code></td>
<td>Height: 1000 Width: 2000</td>
<td><code>?fit=3Dheight&amp;height=3D100</code></td>
<td>Height: 100 Width: 200</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td><code>WIDTH</code></td>
<td>Height: 1000 Width: 2000</td>
<td><code>?fit=3Dwidth&amp;width=3D100</code></td>
<td>Height: 50 Width: 100</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td><code>STRETCH</code></td>
<td>Height: 1000 Width: 2000</td>
<td><code>?fit=3Dstretch&amp;height=3D100&amp;width=3D100</code></td>
<td>Height: 100 Width: 100</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td><code>BOTH</code></td>
<td>Height: 1000 Width: 2000</td>
<td><code>?fit=3Dboth&amp;height=3D100&amp;width=3D100</code></td>
<td>Height: 50 Width: 100</td>
</tr>
</tbody>
</table>
<p><a name=3D"pages-query-parameter"></a></p>
<h3 id=3D"pages-query-parameter">Pages Query Parameter</h3>
<p>Individual pages can be retrieved using the <code>pages</code> query par=
ameter. This is done by adding <code>?pages=3Dvalue</code> at the end of th=
e URL. Currently, only a single page number is supported. Page ranges are n=
ot currently supported. If the page number does not exist or the value prov=
ided is not a single page number, a <code>404 Not Found</code> will be retu=
rned.</p>
<p><a name=3D"default-media-type"></a></p>
<h4 id=3D"default-media-type">Default Media Type</h4>
<p>The ability to retrieve individual pages is only supported by a few file=
 types (PDF, Tiff and text files) when using the default media type (<code>=
*/*</code> or omitted Accept Header). The entire document may be returned w=
hen requesting page 1 and the default media type if a single page cannot be=
 retrieved due to the media type in the response. A <code>404 Not Found</co=
de> will be returned if a single page cannot be retrieved and a page number=
 greater than one is requested.</p>
<p><a name=3D"supported-media-types"></a></p>
<h3 id=3D"supported-media-types">Supported Media Types</h3>
<p><a name=3D"entire-document"></a></p>
<h4 id=3D"entire-document">Entire Document</h4>
